         # find newest failure (max last_trial)\n            newest_failure = max(failures, key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\n            deduped.append(newest_failure)\n    # Now apply capacity trimming\n    # Sort according to priority: failures first, then recency descending, then signature ascending.\n    # Note: In deduped, some entries are successes, some are failures. We need to sort for trimming.\n    # But we must not lose the original order info; we'll sort a copy.\n    sorted_for_trim = sorted(deduped, key=lambda e: (\n        0 if not e['success'] else 1,  # failures (success=False) get 0, successes get 1\n        -e['last_trial'],  # descending recency\n        e['signature']  # ascending signature\n    ))\n    # Keep top capacity\n    trimmed = sorted_for_trim[:capacity]\n    # Restore chronological order (by last_trial ascending)\n    trimmed_sorted = sorted(trimmed, key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\n    # Build memory list (order matters)\n    memory = trimmed_sorted\n    # Build signatures list from memory\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in memory]\n    # Return output\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}\n\nCheck: Are we correctly interpreting \"failures\" as success==False? Yes.\n\nBut note: The output schema requires each memory entry to have \"last_trial\" integer, \"reflection\" string, \"signature\" string, \"success\" boolean. We have those.\n\nNow verify with first case:\nCase 0: capacity=3, trials:\n0: signature e1, reflection r0, success true\n1: e3, r1, false\n2: e0, r2, true\n3: e1, r3, false\n4: e3, r4, false\n5: e1, r5, true\n6: e0, r6, false\n\nEntries: \n0: s=e1, r0, true\n1: s=e3, r1, false\n2: s=e0, r2, true\n3: s=e1, r3, false\n4: s=e3, r4, false\n5: s=e1, r5, true\n6: s=e0, r6, false\n\nGroups:\ne1: [0,3,5] -> failures: 3 (last_trial=3) and? Actually success=False? Trial3: e1, r3, success=false => failure. Trial0 and5 are successes. So failures for e1: [3]. Newest failure is 3.\ne3: [1,4] -> both failures,